Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.690 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it's a hundred and two degrees a pretty  normal Dubai summer evening my brother  and I we see the Golden Navy Chevy Tahoe  parked around the corner even though we  had just finished soccer practice we  make a dash for the AC we know is  waiting for us as we get closer we see  our mom in the driver's seat and our two  cousins in the car waiting for us we  haven't seen them in a couple years so  as soon as we hop in there are those  enthusiastic hellos that you know  teenagers are famous for hey what's up  not much cool  so our mom asks how was soccer practice  good but I'm really tired yeah I know  I have no idea how Cameron is so fit I  noticed our cousin's giving us weird  looks yeah seriously he's the most fit  guy on the team and I've been training  all summer weird right  well my cousins are British and if you  know anything about their lingo my  brother and I had just been describing  how attractive our teammate Cameron was  the same words mean different things to  different people and it doesn't stop  there you'd hear all sorts of  interesting things at an international  High School in Dubai for example talks  about who had the highest quality phone  would get weird looks from our Korean  classmates to them quality meant brand  new or why my Japanese classmates would  start laughing to them quality meant  zero defect the same words mean  different things to different people but  let's be real  calling your teammate hot by accident or  describing your Nokia 3310 as a zero  defect phone  that's kind of funny well sometimes the  different meanings of words aren't so  funny sometimes they lead to distance  between friends distance in our  communities distance in our society and  surprisingly one of these words is  dialogue so some of you are looking at  me the same way my cousin's did that day  and I can't blame you for many in this  room the following definition of  dialogue found in an essay titled the  power of dialogue will resonate it's  defined it as as a discussion aimed at  fostering mutual insights and common  purpose  sounds like a great definition right  well there's an increasing body of  research done by experts like Jacob Hess  that focus on the negative connotations  conservatives hold of this world of this  word it's actually pretty interesting  many people are concerned with the lack  of conservative representation by some  estimates 90% of those involved with the  concept either through funding it  developing it or advocating for it or  liberal so why do I care  well these types of conversation have  been a fabric of my family growing up I  had four grandparents that were all  teachers both my parents have lived all  around the world I have two younger  brothers were 15 months apart and I  really hate to admit this right now  but not only are they taller than me  they're also smarter the youngest is  getting his PhD PhD in Geoscience and  the middle one made the smartest  decision of us all he decided not to go  to grad school all of this is to say we  don't have the Serio typical  relationship with conversations about  values or the word or the world or  politics for us it's exciting and  expected for us to bring our different  perspectives to the dining room table or  during the holidays so with that kind of  upbringing you can imagine how excited I  was to come to the GSB and have these  types of conversations with some of the  smartest people I've ever met that are  all changed about a year ago when  President Trump announced his  immigration ban I was really excited to  understand what my classmates thought of  the issue so I decided to form some  small discussion groups well  fast-forward a couple weeks to Wednesday  February 22nd I'm in Arbuckle dining  hall  talking with one of my more conservative  friends let's call him Jim we're talking  about current events and eventually we  start talking about the ban Wow Jim I  bet a lot of our classmates would love  to hear that perspective you should join  one of the groups I'm putting together  in a couple weeks  sure sounds good who's gonna be there  what are the details I'll list a couple  people all right so what's the format  how is it gonna be structured oh you  know it's gonna be pretty casual during  the evening over pizza it's just gonna  be a dialogue on the issue he grows  silent I had said something wrong I  don't think that's for me sorry oh  really why I don't know if I want to sit  there and be lectured on my views on the  issue no no no this is going to be a  dialogue you know where we try and  understand you yeah I know what dialogue  is I'll pass thanks I probably shouldn't  have been that surprised earlier that  week I'd sent an email to another  conservative friend with the title  finding the right forum for dialogue he  responded with really appreciate the  efforts to open up discussions but don't  believe this is the right place for me  to help sorry so why was my message so  off even though it's not required many  expect dialog to alter their views on  issues if you are in a room and you are  participating usually it's to mediate  conflict and to do that it's usually  required that you find common ground or  you're looking for solutions or  consensus which all require you to  change your views so if the overwhelming  majority of organizers of dialogue are  liberals most of the time they're  looking for common ground and many times  it's expected that participants will  soften or change their views hopefully  now you start to see why conservatives  view this as the tool liberals used to  bring people to their side so what did I  decide to do about it  I decided to dive into the research and  understand why this term was alienating  some of my friends I had lunch with  Keith Hennessey a former adviser to  George W Bush I took a class with James  Fishkin the director for the center of  deliberative democracy I had multiple  calls with interfaith dialogue expert  Randi Paul  if the same words mean different things  to different people research has shown  that reframing the concept of dialogue  can reinforce the healthy norms of  discourse that are practiced on both  sides so armed with this view of the  issue I was able to successfully host  multiple discussion groups last quarter  with not just those two friends but with  several of our conservative classmates I  did so partly by highlighting the  following three benefits of dialogue and  as you try to engage with others and  similar types of interactions I hope you  can remember to mention them as well  expand your knowledge even if you  believe you possess the objective truth  participating in these types of  conversations a lot allows you to test  out your own beliefs and gain new and  interesting perspectives and information  you otherwise would not have received  advocate effectively those that  participate in dialogue are better  missionaries and advocates if the world  doesn't see an issue the way you would  like or in the right way  participating in this discussion will  help you understand why on your story  dialogue is an excellent platform for  people to become their own platform and  not just defined by others or  organizations like mainstream media the  problem is as we shy away from these  types of conversations we allow certain  that we have sulphate false assumptions  that certain groups speak for an entire  movement or that false assumptions can  continue to persist so for example Isis  does not represent all of Islam and  neither does one conservative movement  represent the entire camp this though is  lost when we aren't the ones talking  about our own personal stories in these  types of conversations so different  meanings of words can lead it can lead  to you some funny stories and sometimes  they can lead to distance while other  times they're the foundation of a  country we hold these truths to be  self-evident that all men are created  equal that they are endowed by their  creator with certain unalienable rights  the phrase all men are created equal  means something different to us today  then it mean meant to our founding  fathers  and that's okay they had different ideas  different assumptions different  connotations but they worked through it  they did so by defining those  differences and the benefits of them  today let's do the same thing let's  realize that dialogue can mean different  things to different people but better  yet let's reframe the concept in a more  inclusive way so we all want to  participate doing so will literally make  us a better country and by the way is it  just me or is the word literally  starting to mean something differently  yes thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

